# mail2 env — copy to `.env` for local overrides. None of these are required:
# the app boots without this file. The dev script loads `.env` itself
# (`node --env-file-if-exists=.env`, because SvelteKit does not put `.env`
# values into process.env), so restart the dev server after changing it.
# Real environment variables win over `.env` values.

# Mail 2.0 signs in through its own /login page. With nothing set below, the
# password-login fallback is used: credentials go via HTTP Basic to the JMAP
# server and the password is AES-sealed in the server-side session store —
# works out of the box for local dev against the live mail server.
#
# Recommended (what production runs): register an OAuth client in Stalwart
# (client id + redirect URI) and enable the block — then the session stores
# tokens, never the password. Same tri-state rollback flag as webmail 1.0.
#
# STALWART_OAUTH_ENABLED=true
# STALWART_OAUTH_ISSUER_URL=https://mail.zaur.app
# STALWART_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=zaur-mail2-dev
# STALWART_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5175/api/auth/oauth/callback
# STALWART_OAUTH_SCOPES=offline_access urn:ietf:params:oauth:scope:mail urn:ietf:params:oauth:scope:contacts urn:ietf:params:oauth:scope:calendars
# STALWART_PASSWORD_LOGIN_ROLLBACK_ENABLED=   # unset → on unless OAuth is enabled; true/false force it

# Session sharing with webmail 1.0 (optional fallback — mail2 no longer
# depends on it, but a 1.0 login still lands you in mail2 when both apps
# point at the same store). The session cookie holds an id that each app
# looks up in ONE SQLite store; by default that store is per-app
# (`<cwd>/.data/store.sqlite`). To share with webmail locally, point at its
# store — relative to this app's directory:
STORE_DB_PATH=../webmail/.data/store.sqlite

# Only for tunnel/hostname testing (README → "Testing over the network"): the
# parent-domain cookie that makes the login from webmail-dev.zaur.app visible
# on mail2-dev.zaur.app. While set, plain localhost logins BREAK (browsers
# reject a .zaur.app cookie coming from localhost), so keep it commented
# except during a tunnel test — prefer passing it inline on the command line:
# SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.zaur.app

# Required only when NODE_ENV=production. Session records in the store are
# sealed with it — two apps sharing one store must share the same value.
# SESSION_SECRET=

# Optional: JMAP server used for proxied requests. Defaults to the server URL
# stored in the session account (the live mail.zaur.app in dev).
# JMAP_INTERNAL_URL=

# Optional: where the login page's "Create your account" link points
# (account creation stays with the register service).
# PUBLIC_REGISTER_URL=https://register.zaur.app

# Optional: the register service's HMAC-signed internal API, which owns the
# recovery email used for password resets. With both set, Settings → Security
# offers a "Recovery email" card; unset, the card is simply not shown. Same
# values webmail 1.0 uses.
# REGISTER_INTERNAL_URL=http://register:3000
# REGISTER_INTERNAL_SECRET=

# Rate limiting reads the client address from X-Forwarded-For counting this
# many trusted proxy hops from the right (1 = Traefik; 2 if a CDN is fronted).
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1
